latest_plan = "Based on our refined understanding, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Create a deep copy of the input grid:\n   - This ensures we don't modify the original input.\n\n2. Initialize an auxiliary grid (let's call it 'open_grid'):\n   - Same dimensions as the input grid.\n   - All cells initially set to False.\n   - This grid will track which cells have a path to the edge.\n\n3. Perform a flood fill operation from the edges:\n   - Start from every cell on the grid's perimeter.\n   - If the cell is not blue (1) or red (2), mark it as 'open' in the open_grid.\n   - Propagate the 'open' status to neighboring cells (including diagonals) that are not blue or red.\n   - Continue this process until no more cells can be marked as 'open'.\n\n4. Fill enclosed areas:\n   - Iterate through all cells in the grid.\n   - If a cell is black (0) in the original grid and False in the open_grid, change it to yellow (4) in the output grid.\n   - Leave all other cells unchanged.\n\n5. Return the modified grid.\n\nDetailed algorithm for the flood fill operation (step 3):\n\na. Create a stack or queue to manage cells to be processed.\nb. For each cell on the grid's perimeter:\n   - If the cell is not blue (1) or red (2), add it to the stack/queue.\nc. While the stack/queue is not empty:\n   - Pop a cell from the stack/queue.\n   - If the cell is within grid bounds, not blue or red, and not already marked as 'open':\n     * Mark it as 'open' in the open_grid.\n     * Add all eight neighboring cells (including diagonals) to the stack/queue.\n\nThis plan addresses all the key aspects of the problem:\n- It preserves blue lines and red squares.\n- It correctly identifies enclosed areas by checking connectivity to the edges.\n- It handles both orthogonal and diagonal connections.\n- It works for various grid sizes and configurations.\n\nThe flood fill approach ensures that we correctly identify all cells that have a path to the edge, regardless of how convoluted that path might be. Any cell not reached by this flood fill is, by definition, enclosed and should be filled with yellow."
